<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Eretria</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Eretria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Euboean city that helped found Greek colonies in Italy and the north Aegean, and whose support for the Ionian Revolt brought Persian destruction in 490 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eretria grew on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, in the 8th century BCE, possibly succeeding the earlier settlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lefkandi">Lefkandi</a>. With <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> it was a pioneer of long-distance Greek trade, and their rivalry over the Lelantine plain became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelantine-war">Lelantine War</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Eretrians joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithekoussai">Pithekoussai</a> and settled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/methone-pieria">Methone</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a> and, by tradition, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corcyra</a> before the Corinthians. It was ruled by aristocracies of horse-breeders. Eretria sent five ships to help <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/miletus">Miletus</a> in 499 BCE, sharing in the burning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. Its later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> are among the best-preserved on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> sacked Eretria in 490 BCE and deported its people. Rebuilt, it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> and later hosted the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menedemus-of-eretria">Menedemus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sacked it in 198 BCE, and it lost independence in 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eretria.jpg" alt="Eretria" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Apaleutos25 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%95%CF%81%CE%AD%CF%84%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%B1,_Eretria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Eretria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Menedemus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Apollo Daphnephoros</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Eretreia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=eretria" aria-label="Eretria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5272 799 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Eretria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5592 1055L6140 1251M5592 1055L5591 1068M5592 1055L5586 1053"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5586 1053h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 1055h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 1055h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=eretria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

